  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nStout and expanding H5 ridging and surface high pressure over\nthe southeast CONUS continue to advect very warm temperatures\nand elevated humidity levels into the region. Despite some mid-\nlevel cloud cover, temperatures will approach or exceed record \nlevels this afternoon. Increasing dew point temperatures into \nthe 60s will yield peak heat index values around the middle to \nupper 90s, which may cause some heat related impacts. SPS for \nheat-related impacts remains in effect for the western half of \nthe region until 22z. \n\nTonight, convection will develop over the southern Great Plains\nand Ozarks as a surface low develops and moves northeast through\nthe Midwest along a frontal boundary positioned north and west\nof the area. Lingering convection will move through the region \nWednesday morning and early afternoon. Depending on how much \nclearing we week, development is possible Wednesday afternoon \nand evening. A few strong to severe storms cannot be ruled out \nif this occurred due to the presence of modest 0-6 km shear and \ninstability. Any strong storms that do develop would pose a \ndamaging wind risk. SPC has introduced a marginal risk for the \nentire forecast area for this potential. \n\nThursday may see a bit of a lull for areas south of I-64 as the\nfrontal boundary lifts north of the region. Clouds will keep\ntemperatures in the lower to middle 80s, but dew point\ntemperatures will be very muggy by mid-October standards in the\nlower to middle 70s. Thursday night into Friday morning, the\ncold front will sweep through the region as a deep H5 ridge\nmoves through the northern tier of the CONUS. Cannot rule out a\nstrongly-forced segment of strong/gusty storms along the leading\nedge of the front, especially along I-64, where moisture and\ninstability will be highest. SPC's Day 3 outlook does have\nanother marginal risk that clips this area. Rainfall totals for\nthe Wednesday-Thursday period look pretty modest, generally\nabout 0.25-0.75\". \n\nFriday through next weekend will see much cooler conditions and\na return to dry weather. Gradient winds will be quiet gusty\n(gusts up to 25-35 mph) on Friday following the frontal passage,\nand this could lead to elevated fire danger for areas that miss\nout on wetting rains in the next two days. Additionally, the\nfirst widespread frosts of the season are possible Saturday\nmorning and especially Sunday morning. However, the cooldown\nwill be short-lived, as H5 ridging will increase over the region\nearly next week, and high temperatures will warm 5-10 degrees \nabove normal by next Monday-Tuesday.",
